# Line endings are preserved for files whose bytes matter to the consumer.
#
# The container entrypoint is executed by /bin/sh inside a Linux image. With
# core.autocrlf=true (the default on Windows) git would store and check it out with CRLF,
# and the container would fail at start-up with a message that has nothing to do with the
# script ("not found" pointing at the shebang).
docker/*.sh text eol=lf

# The WSL helper scripts are executed by bash inside the Ubuntu distro the Detox suite runs
# in. With CRLF endings bash fails on the FIRST line with `set: -: invalid option` — an error
# that says nothing about line endings, and that kept the whole e2e runbook behind a syntax
# error. They were committed with CRLF and could not run at all; pin them to LF here so a
# checkout on Windows (core.autocrlf=true) still produces files Linux can execute.
econoflow-mobile/scripts/wsl/*.sh text eol=lf
